SUPPY CHAIN
The chocolate supply chain to Europe is a complex journey starting
primarily in West Africa (C=C3=B4te d'Ivoire, Ghana) and South America, mov=
ing
through agricultural processing, international shipping, European
manufacturing, and finally retail distribution. Each step is rigorously
monitored to meet European Union Deforestation Regulation (EUDR) standards.
[1 <https://www.cbi.eu/market-information/cocoa/what-demand>, 2
<https://www.aveva.com/en/our-industrial-life/type/article/chocolate-produc=
tion-and-the-challenge-of-supply-chain-optimization/>,
3 <https://www.cbi.eu/market-information/cocoa/trends>, 4
<https://abbottblackstone.eu/blog/from-bean-to-business-inside-the-modern-c=
acao-supply-chain/>
]
1. Cultivation & Primary Processing (Origin Countries)

   - *Farming:* Smallholder farmers grow cacao trees on 2-4 hectare plots
   in tropical regions. [1
   <https://worldcocoafoundation.org/from-bean-to-bar>]
   - *Harvest & Fermentation:* Cocoa pods are harvested, split open, and
   beans are fermented in heaps for 5=E2=80=937 days to develop foundationa=
l flavor
   profiles. [1
   <https://www.weforum.org/stories/2020/11/cocoa-chocolate-supply-chain-bu=
siness-bar-africa-exports/>,
   2
   <https://abbottblackstone.eu/blog/from-bean-to-business-inside-the-moder=
n-cacao-supply-chain/>
   ]
   - *Drying & Sorting:* Beans are sun-dried and collected by local
   cooperatives or buying agents for quality assessment. [1
   <https://www.ebrd.com/content/dam/ebrd_dxp/assets/pdfs/financial-institu=
tion/sustainable-finance/environmental-and-social-risk-management/issues/Co=
coa-sector-supply-chain-guidance.pdf>,
   2
   <https://www.cbi.eu/market-information/cocoa-cocoa-products/chocolate/ma=
rket-entry>,
   3 <https://worldcocoafoundation.org/from-bean-to-bar>]
   - *Regulatory Compliance:* Under the EU Deforestation Regulation (EUDR)
   <https://www.cbi.eu/market-information/cocoa/what-demand>, farmers and
   exporters must submit GPS-mapped polygons to prove the cocoa is
   deforestation-free and legal. [1
   <https://www.cbi.eu/market-information/cocoa/what-demand>, 2
   <https://abbottblackstone.eu/blog/from-bean-to-business-inside-the-moder=
n-cacao-supply-chain/>
   ]

2. Export & Industrial Processing

   - *Exporting:* Cleaned and packaged beans (often in jute bags) are
   exported to Europe.
   - *Winnowing & Roasting:* Major processing hubs (often in countries like
   the Netherlands or Germany) roast, winnow, and grind the beans.
   - *Liquor & Pressing:* This creates intermediary products: cocoa liquor,
   cocoa butter, and cocoa powder.
   - *Learn more about the industrial supply chain via this AVEVA
   Industrial Supply Chain Overview
   <https://www.aveva.com/en/our-industrial-life/type/article/chocolate-pro=
duction-and-the-challenge-of-supply-chain-optimization/>.*
   [1
   <https://www.cbi.eu/market-information/cocoa-cocoa-products/chocolate/ma=
rket-potential>,
   2
   <https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0924224416301868=
>,
   3
   <https://www.weforum.org/stories/2020/11/cocoa-chocolate-supply-chain-bu=
siness-bar-africa-exports/>,
   4 <https://worldcocoafoundation.org/from-bean-to-bar>]

3. International Logistics

   - *Sea Freight:* Raw cocoa and semi-finished products are transported to
   Europe via sea freight, usually taking around 26 days from West Africa. =
[
   1
   <https://www.maersk.com/news/articles/2021/12/15/supply-chain-visibility=
-for-your-chocolates>
   ]
   - *Temperature Control:* Because raw cocoa is highly sensitive to
   moisture and temperature fluctuations, containers require thermal blanke=
ts
   and strict climate control to prevent spoilage. [1
   <https://foodvoices.co.uk/2024/03/supply-chain-logistics-for-chocolate>,
   2
   <https://www.maersk.com/news/articles/2021/12/15/supply-chain-visibility=
-for-your-chocolates>
   ]
   - *Read about Sensitech Transportation Best Practices
   <https://www.sensitech.com/en/blog/blog-articles/blog-transporting-wareh=
ousing-chocolates.html>
   for preventing quality loss during transit.*

4. European Manufacturing

   - *Blending & Conching:* Manufacturers (like Barry Callebaut, Nestl=C3=
=A9, or
   Mondelez) mix cocoa butter, cocoa liquor, and powder with milk, sugar, a=
nd
   emulsifiers. [1
   <https://www.confectioneryproduction.com/blog/48566/guest-blog-exploring=
-the-dynamics-of-the-global-cocoa-supply-chain/>,
   2 <https://worldcocoafoundation.org/from-bean-to-bar>]
   - *Molding & Packaging:* The liquid chocolate is conched (to smooth
   texture), tempered, and molded into final consumer goods (bars, spreads,
   seasonal items). [1 <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3DPaEnbL-fT78>, 2
   <https://www.culinaryartsswitzerland.com/en/news/chocolate-making-proces=
s/>
   ]
   - *European Hubs:* Countries like *Germany, Italy, Belgium, and Poland*
   serve as the primary manufacturing centers, dominating European trade an=
d
   exporting high volumes of finished chocolate. [1
   <https://www.visualcapitalist.com/cocoa-a-bittersweet-supply-chain/>, 2
   <https://www.cbi.eu/market-information/cocoa-cocoa-products/chocolate/ma=
rket-potential>
   ]

5. Retail & Distribution

   - *B2B Distribution:* Finished products or bulk couvertures are sold to
   chocolatiers, bakeries, or retail networks.
   - *Specialty Craft Markets:* For premium or bean-to-bar products,
   specialized distributors act as the main gateway to stores and online sh=
ops
   across the UK and the Netherlands.
   - *Find B2B wholesale buyers and distributors via Wholesale
   Confectionery Hub's Guide
   <https://wholesaleconfectioneryhub.com/blog/chocolate-wholesale-europe>.=
*
   [1
   <https://www.cbi.eu/market-information/cocoa-cocoa-products/bean-bar/mar=
ket-entry>,
   2
   <https://www.ebrd.com/content/dam/ebrd_dxp/assets/pdfs/financial-institu=
tion/sustainable-finance/environmental-and-social-risk-management/issues/Co=
coa-sector-supply-chain-guidance.pdf>
   ]

